{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Q5QdAjexEiJ9FT1EgctyAo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CT2XP3M4JYg9N1MGFb4ERv","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Roundtable Step 2-4: convergence","prompt":"You are the Review Chair conducting a roundtable discussion for a paper review. Two reviewers have submitted independent reviews. Your job is to run Steps 2-4 of the roundtable: cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis.\n\n## Paper\n\"Exception Events Are the Minimal Sufficient Signal for Trace-Based Verdict Prediction\"\n- Component ablation of execution trace representations for verdict prediction (pass/fail)\n- Exception-only SFT achieves 99.83% on 4 models (1.5B-13B)\n- Three claims: exception dominance, information density bottleneck, two-tier hierarchy\n- 11 pages, HumanEval+MBPP dataset, LoRA fine-tuning\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments): Score 3/6, Confidence 4/5\n\n**Key Strengths:**\n1. Comprehensive ablation design (7+ representations × 4 models × multi-seed)\n2. Statistical rigor above average (Wilson CIs, paired bootstrap, McNemar tests)\n3. Transparent reporting of negative results (DeepSeek outlier, GeneratorExit errors, VOTA truncation confound)\n4. Regex baseline comparison addresses \"is SFT necessary?\" question\n5. Cross-dataset eval on DebugBench\n6. Calibration + error orthogonality analyses add interpretive value\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n1. **Core finding near-tautological**: ~100% of fail-class samples contain exceptions. Exception presence ≈ label. Regex B5 achieves 99.33%, SFT marginal gain is 0.50pp (6 samples). The paper doesn't report fail-class exception rate.\n2. **Potential label leakage via format markers**: Internal artifact flagged 70 silent logic errors (20.6% of fail) with placeholder text differences between pass/fail. Not discussed.\n3. **74%/26% decomposition unreplicated**: From single model, single seed. Used 4+ times in paper.\n4. **Component ablation (ae_only, loop_only) on one model only**: Two-tier hierarchy claim is single-model.\n5. **Effective sample size**: 48.99% duplicates → N_eff=607, but CIs use N=1190.\n6. **Phase transition is expected**: Just the point where tail-truncation captures/misses the exception traceback. Not a novel finding about information density.\n7. **DeepSeek seed sensitivity**: 1/3 seeds at 91.09%, but paper reports 2/3-seed median as primary result.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- What fraction of fail-class samples have exceptions?\n- How are the 70 silent error samples handled?\n- Can 74/26 decomposition be replicated on DeepSeek?\n- Why not test ae_only/loop_only on more models?\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work): Score 3/6, Confidence 4/5\n\n**Key Strengths:**\n1. Thorough experimental design with meaningful ablations\n2. Cross-model breadth reasonable (4 models, 3 architectures, 8.7x param range)\n3. Phase transition finding genuinely interesting\n4. Honest transparent reporting\n5. Calibration analysis adds second evidence dimension\n6. Related work improved to ~30 references\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n1. **Limited novelty**: Exceptions predict failure ≈ tautology from SE perspective. Conceptual advance thin.\n2. **Task significance unclear**: If trace available, verdict already known from execution. Circular dependency undermines practical motivation.\n3. **Single dataset, narrow fault distribution**: HumanEval+MBPP short algorithmic problems. LLM-generated solutions dominated by standard exceptions. Universal claims from narrow evidence.\n4. **\"Richer traces hurt\" overclaimed**: VOTA vs exception_only = 5 samples difference. DeepSeek VOTA gap confounded by truncation.\n5. **Claim calibration issues**: Title/abstract make universal claims tested on one task, one language, one fault type.\n6. **Missing important references**: Defects4J, mutation testing literature, test oracle literature, Seeker (ICLR 2025), Code Execution as Grounded Supervision (EMNLP 2025).\n7. **Two-tier hierarchy is descriptive, not generalizable**: Expected by construction that direct fault indicators outperform indirect behavioral patterns.\n8. **Statistical power at ceiling**: 2 errors total, 5-sample differences between key conditions.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- What deployment scenario needs learned predictor but not execution result?\n- What fraction of failures are exception vs. silent logic errors?\n- How much SFT performance comes from format marker shortcut?\n- Why ae_only/loop_only only on Qwen-7B?\n\n## Your Task\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where R1 and R2 agree, disagree, or bring complementary concerns.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nDetermine which concerns are:\n- **Major (consensus)**: Both reviewers flag or would agree on importance\n- **Major (one reviewer, validated)**: One reviewer raises, the other's evidence supports\n- **Minor**: Real but not paper-breaking\n- **Dismissed**: Raised but not well-supported on reflection\n\n### Step 4: Synthesis\nProduce the final consolidated review with:\n1. Paper summary (3-4 sentences)\n2. Consolidated strengths (numbered)\n3. Consolidated weaknesses — categorize as Major or Minor (numbered)\n4. Key questions for authors (numbered)\n5. Missing references\n6. **Final Score**: X/6 with justification\n7. **Confidence**: X/5\n8. **Meta-review recommendation**: Accept / Borderline / Reject with 2-sentence rationale\n\nUse the 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTop venue, ~25% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10290,"cache_read_input_tokens":51823,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10290},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"801e815f-639b-4202-aae5-0f4f3a5c9365","uuid":"18b270f6-6943-4cec-9c65-75e5d806542b"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"afb282ab136748275","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CT2XP3M4JYg9N1MGFb4ERv","description":"Roundtable Step 2-4: convergence","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are the Review Chair conducting a roundtable discussion for a paper review. Two reviewers have submitted independent reviews. Your job is to run Steps 2-4 of the roundtable: cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis.\n\n## Paper\n\"Exception Events Are the Minimal Sufficient Signal for Trace-Based Verdict Prediction\"\n- Component ablation of execution trace representations for verdict prediction (pass/fail)\n- Exception-only SFT achieves 99.83% on 4 models (1.5B-13B)\n- Three claims: exception dominance, information density bottleneck, two-tier hierarchy\n- 11 pages, HumanEval+MBPP dataset, LoRA fine-tuning\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments): Score 3/6, Confidence 4/5\n\n**Key Strengths:**\n1. Comprehensive ablation design (7+ representations × 4 models × multi-seed)\n2. Statistical rigor above average (Wilson CIs, paired bootstrap, McNemar tests)\n3. Transparent reporting of negative results (DeepSeek outlier, GeneratorExit errors, VOTA truncation confound)\n4. Regex baseline comparison addresses \"is SFT necessary?\" question\n5. Cross-dataset eval on DebugBench\n6. Calibration + error orthogonality analyses add interpretive value\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n1. **Core finding near-tautological**: ~100% of fail-class samples contain exceptions. Exception presence ≈ label. Regex B5 achieves 99.33%, SFT marginal gain is 0.50pp (6 samples). The paper doesn't report fail-class exception rate.\n2. **Potential label leakage via format markers**: Internal artifact flagged 70 silent logic errors (20.6% of fail) with placeholder text differences between pass/fail. Not discussed.\n3. **74%/26% decomposition unreplicated**: From single model, single seed. Used 4+ times in paper.\n4. **Component ablation (ae_only, loop_only) on one model only**: Two-tier hierarchy claim is single-model.\n5. **Effective sample size**: 48.99% duplicates → N_eff=607, but CIs use N=1190.\n6. **Phase transition is expected**: Just the point where tail-truncation captures/misses the exception traceback. Not a novel finding about information density.\n7. **DeepSeek seed sensitivity**: 1/3 seeds at 91.09%, but paper reports 2/3-seed median as primary result.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- What fraction of fail-class samples have exceptions?\n- How are the 70 silent error samples handled?\n- Can 74/26 decomposition be replicated on DeepSeek?\n- Why not test ae_only/loop_only on more models?\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work): Score 3/6, Confidence 4/5\n\n**Key Strengths:**\n1. Thorough experimental design with meaningful ablations\n2. Cross-model breadth reasonable (4 models, 3 architectures, 8.7x param range)\n3. Phase transition finding genuinely interesting\n4. Honest transparent reporting\n5. Calibration analysis adds second evidence dimension\n6. Related work improved to ~30 references\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n1. **Limited novelty**: Exceptions predict failure ≈ tautology from SE perspective. Conceptual advance thin.\n2. **Task significance unclear**: If trace available, verdict already known from execution. Circular dependency undermines practical motivation.\n3. **Single dataset, narrow fault distribution**: HumanEval+MBPP short algorithmic problems. LLM-generated solutions dominated by standard exceptions. Universal claims from narrow evidence.\n4. **\"Richer traces hurt\" overclaimed**: VOTA vs exception_only = 5 samples difference. DeepSeek VOTA gap confounded by truncation.\n5. **Claim calibration issues**: Title/abstract make universal claims tested on one task, one language, one fault type.\n6. **Missing important references**: Defects4J, mutation testing literature, test oracle literature, Seeker (ICLR 2025), Code Execution as Grounded Supervision (EMNLP 2025).\n7. **Two-tier hierarchy is descriptive, not generalizable**: Expected by construction that direct fault indicators outperform indirect behavioral patterns.\n8. **Statistical power at ceiling**: 2 errors total, 5-sample differences between key conditions.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- What deployment scenario needs learned predictor but not execution result?\n- What fraction of failures are exception vs. silent logic errors?\n- How much SFT performance comes from format marker shortcut?\n- Why ae_only/loop_only only on Qwen-7B?\n\n## Your Task\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where R1 and R2 agree, disagree, or bring complementary concerns.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nDetermine which concerns are:\n- **Major (consensus)**: Both reviewers flag or would agree on importance\n- **Major (one reviewer, validated)**: One reviewer raises, the other's evidence supports\n- **Minor**: Real but not paper-breaking\n- **Dismissed**: Raised but not well-supported on reflection\n\n### Step 4: Synthesis\nProduce the final consolidated review with:\n1. Paper summary (3-4 sentences)\n2. Consolidated strengths (numbered)\n3. Consolidated weaknesses — categorize as Major or Minor (numbered)\n4. Key questions for authors (numbered)\n5. Missing references\n6. **Final Score**: X/6 with justification\n7. **Confidence**: X/5\n8. **Meta-review recommendation**: Accept / Borderline / Reject with 2-sentence rationale\n\nUse the 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTop venue, ~25% acceptance rate.","uuid":"afe94df0-5fc7-4457-b49a-fa2c2a831de1","session_id":"801e815f-639b-4202-aae5-0f4f3a5c9365"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are the Review Chair conducting a roundtable discussion for a paper review. Two reviewers have submitted independent reviews. Your job is to run Steps 2-4 of the roundtable: cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis.\n\n## Paper\n\"Exception Events Are the Minimal Sufficient Signal for Trace-Based Verdict Prediction\"\n- Component ablation of execution trace representations for verdict prediction (pass/fail)\n- Exception-only SFT achieves 99.83% on 4 models (1.5B-13B)\n- Three claims: exception dominance, information density bottleneck, two-tier hierarchy\n- 11 pages, HumanEval+MBPP dataset, LoRA fine-tuning\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments): Score 3/6, Confidence 4/5\n\n**Key Strengths:**\n1. Comprehensive ablation design (7+ representations × 4 models × multi-seed)\n2. Statistical rigor above average (Wilson CIs, paired bootstrap, McNemar tests)\n3. Transparent reporting of negative results (DeepSeek outlier, GeneratorExit errors, VOTA truncation confound)\n4. Regex baseline comparison addresses \"is SFT necessary?\" question\n5. Cross-dataset eval on DebugBench\n6. Calibration + error orthogonality analyses add interpretive value\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n1. **Core finding near-tautological**: ~100% of fail-class samples contain exceptions. Exception presence ≈ label. Regex B5 achieves 99.33%, SFT marginal gain is 0.50pp (6 samples). The paper doesn't report fail-class exception rate.\n2. **Potential label leakage via format markers**: Internal artifact flagged 70 silent logic errors (20.6% of fail) with placeholder text differences between pass/fail. Not discussed.\n3. **74%/26% decomposition unreplicated**: From single model, single seed. Used 4+ times in paper.\n4. **Component ablation (ae_only, loop_only) on one model only**: Two-tier hierarchy claim is single-model.\n5. **Effective sample size**: 48.99% duplicates → N_eff=607, but CIs use N=1190.\n6. **Phase transition is expected**: Just the point where tail-truncation captures/misses the exception traceback. Not a novel finding about information density.\n7. **DeepSeek seed sensitivity**: 1/3 seeds at 91.09%, but paper reports 2/3-seed median as primary result.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- What fraction of fail-class samples have exceptions?\n- How are the 70 silent error samples handled?\n- Can 74/26 decomposition be replicated on DeepSeek?\n- Why not test ae_only/loop_only on more models?\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work): Score 3/6, Confidence 4/5\n\n**Key Strengths:**\n1. Thorough experimental design with meaningful ablations\n2. Cross-model breadth reasonable (4 models, 3 architectures, 8.7x param range)\n3. Phase transition finding genuinely interesting\n4. Honest transparent reporting\n5. Calibration analysis adds second evidence dimension\n6. Related work improved to ~30 references\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n1. **Limited novelty**: Exceptions predict failure ≈ tautology from SE perspective. Conceptual advance thin.\n2. **Task significance unclear**: If trace available, verdict already known from execution. Circular dependency undermines practical motivation.\n3. **Single dataset, narrow fault distribution**: HumanEval+MBPP short algorithmic problems. LLM-generated solutions dominated by standard exceptions. Universal claims from narrow evidence.\n4. **\"Richer traces hurt\" overclaimed**: VOTA vs exception_only = 5 samples difference. DeepSeek VOTA gap confounded by truncation.\n5. **Claim calibration issues**: Title/abstract make universal claims tested on one task, one language, one fault type.\n6. **Missing important references**: Defects4J, mutation testing literature, test oracle literature, Seeker (ICLR 2025), Code Execution as Grounded Supervision (EMNLP 2025).\n7. **Two-tier hierarchy is descriptive, not generalizable**: Expected by construction that direct fault indicators outperform indirect behavioral patterns.\n8. **Statistical power at ceiling**: 2 errors total, 5-sample differences between key conditions.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- What deployment scenario needs learned predictor but not execution result?\n- What fraction of failures are exception vs. silent logic errors?\n- How much SFT performance comes from format marker shortcut?\n- Why ae_only/loop_only only on Qwen-7B?\n\n## Your Task\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where R1 and R2 agree, disagree, or bring complementary concerns.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nDetermine which concerns are:\n- **Major (consensus)**: Both reviewers flag or would agree on importance\n- **Major (one reviewer, validated)**: One reviewer raises, the other's evidence supports\n- **Minor**: Real but not paper-breaking\n- **Dismissed**: Raised but not well-supported on reflection\n\n### Step 4: Synthesis\nProduce the final consolidated review with:\n1. Paper summary (3-4 sentences)\n2. Consolidated strengths (numbered)\n3. Consolidated weaknesses — categorize as Major or Minor (numbered)\n4. Key questions for authors (numbered)\n5. Missing references\n6. **Final Score**: X/6 with justification\n7. **Confidence**: X/5\n8. **Meta-review recommendation**: Accept / Borderline / Reject with 2-sentence rationale\n\nUse the 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTop venue, ~25% acceptance rate."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CT2XP3M4JYg9N1MGFb4ERv","session_id":"801e815f-639b-4202-aae5-0f4f3a5c9365","uuid":"1c174cae-e9b7-4428-8aa4-713f0ae151e4","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:57:35.146Z"}
